WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.600
We present DS-1000,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.780
a natural and reliable benchmark for data science
code generation.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.960
DS-1000 gathers a thousand real problems across
seven widely used Python libraries such as NumPy and Pandas,

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.140
all drawn from StackOverflow.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.320
It pairs these realistic problems with reliable
execution-based evaluation and a built-in

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.500
defense against memorization,

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:29.680
giving the community a trustworthy yardstick for
data science coding models.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.960
Data science coding is central to many fields,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.984
yet it demands fluency in specialized libraries
that create real barriers for everyday users.

00:00:39.064 --> 00:00:42.223
Code generation models could lower those barriers,

00:00:42.303 --> 00:00:46.240
but the community lacked a benchmark to measure
real progress.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.199
Most existing datasets focus on competitive or
interview-style problems that test algorithms

00:00:52.279 --> 00:00:54.078
rather than real-world usage,

00:00:54.158 --> 00:01:01.398
and many score answers with surface-form metrics
like BLEU that drift away from what programmers actually intend.

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:06.904
There was no benchmark combining natural, richly
contextual problems with a reliable,

00:01:06.984 --> 00:01:09.560
execution-based way to judge correctness.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.526
Real data science questions rarely look like clean
textbook prompts.

00:01:14.606 --> 00:01:19.123
On StackOverflow, users describe messy contexts:
their broken code,

00:01:19.203 --> 00:01:24.131
the error they hit, and concrete input-output
examples of what they want.

00:01:24.211 --> 00:01:27.013
Prior benchmarks strip that richness away.

00:01:27.093 --> 00:01:29.689
At the same time, as models get better,

00:01:29.769 --> 00:01:32.983
surface-form scores like BLEU become misleading,

00:01:33.063 --> 00:01:36.345
rewarding text that looks right but does not run.

00:01:36.425 --> 00:01:43.000
This motivates a benchmark built from natural
problems and judged by actually executing the code.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.342
DS-1000 makes three contributions.

00:01:47.422 --> 00:01:51.384
First, it provides a thousand realistic problems
with diverse contexts,

00:01:51.464 --> 00:01:55.426
adapted from naturally occurring StackOverflow
questions across seven popular Python libraries.

00:01:55.506 --> 00:01:59.468
Second,

00:01:59.548 --> 00:02:03.510
it implements reliable multi-criteria evaluation
that runs test cases and checks surface-form constraints,

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:07.552
so accepted solutions are almost always genuinely correct.

00:02:07.632 --> 00:02:11.594
Third,

00:02:11.674 --> 00:02:15.636
it proactively defends against memorization by
perturbing problems so models cannot simply

00:02:15.716 --> 00:02:19.678
recall pre-training answers.

00:02:19.758 --> 00:02:23.720
The authors release the benchmark and use it to
evaluate five state-of-the-art code models.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.131
DS-1000 is built through a five-stage pipeline.

00:02:27.211 --> 00:02:30.573
Annotators first select high-vote, testable, useful,

00:02:30.653 --> 00:02:35.272
and representative StackOverflow problems and
rewrite them for clarity.

00:02:35.352 --> 00:02:41.692
They add a code context with insertion markers
showing exactly where the model must fill in code.

00:02:41.772 --> 00:02:47.451
They then implement automatic tests that check
functional correctness by executing test

00:02:47.531 --> 00:02:50.628
cases and also enforce surface-form constraints,

00:02:50.708 --> 00:02:54.798
such as forbidding certain APIs or keywords in the
syntax tree.

00:02:54.878 --> 00:02:59.232
To defend against memorization, they perturb each
original problem,

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:06.778
using surface perturbations that leave the
reference solution unchanged and semantic perturbations that change it,

00:03:06.858 --> 00:03:09.227
plus deliberately difficult rewrites.

00:03:09.307 --> 00:03:14.720
Finally they red-team the evaluation, requiring it
to reject known-wrong solutions.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.280
Every problem, solution, and metric is reviewed by
at least three expert annotators.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:27.690
DS-1000 contains a thousand problems originating
from four hundred fifty-one unique StackOverflow posts,

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:33.159
spanning seven widely used Python libraries: NumPy,
Pandas, TensorFlow, PyTorch,

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:35.620
SciPy, Scikit-learn, and Matplotlib.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:41.500
More than half of the problems are modified from
their sources to resist memorization,

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.191
including one hundred fifty-two surface perturbations,

00:03:45.271 --> 00:03:48.404
two hundred thirty-five semantic perturbations,

00:03:48.484 --> 00:03:51.481
and one hundred sixty-two difficult rewrites.

00:03:51.561 --> 00:03:56.813
On average each problem has one point six test
cases, one hundred forty words,

00:03:56.893 --> 00:04:00.231
and a reference solution of three point six lines,

00:04:00.311 --> 00:04:03.855
and about one in five carry surface-form constraints.

00:04:03.935 --> 00:04:07.068
With a hundred forty average words per problem,

00:04:07.148 --> 00:04:11.580
DS-1000 is far richer in context than other data
science datasets.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.779
DS-1000 clearly separates models of different strength.

00:04:15.859 --> 00:04:19.890
The best public system, Codex-002 in the insertion format,

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:24.285
reaches only forty-three point three percent
average accuracy,

00:04:24.365 --> 00:04:29.460
nontrivial but far from solved, leaving substantial
room for improvement.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.224
Across five models from three families,

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:38.179
accuracy ranges from seven point four percent up to
forty-three point three percent.

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:41.865
Insertion format, which supplies right-hand context,

00:04:41.945 --> 00:04:46.827
gives Codex-002 a four point one percent boost over
completion format,

00:04:46.907 --> 00:04:51.080
underscoring the value of infilling for data
science coding.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:57.460
Weaker models such as CodeGen-6B and InCoder-6B
fall below five percent on some libraries.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.975
To show why memorization matters,

00:05:00.055 --> 00:05:03.466
the authors probe the popular numpy-100 problem set.

00:05:03.546 --> 00:05:07.091
Codex-002 scores seventy-two point five percent there,

00:05:07.171 --> 00:05:11.723
but accuracy collapses to forty point six percent
after perturbation,

00:05:11.803 --> 00:05:17.161
and in thirty-six percent of semantic cases the
model still returns the original,

00:05:17.241 --> 00:05:18.571
now-incorrect answer,

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:23.404
evidence that it is recalling memorized solutions
rather than reasoning.

00:05:23.484 --> 00:05:28.640
On DS-1000 the perturbation drop is much gentler,
about three to nine percent,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.862
because these problems appear less often online.

00:05:31.942 --> 00:05:37.770
This confirms perturbation as a practical defense
against memorization by future models.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.151
A few numbers capture DS-1000.

00:05:40.231 --> 00:05:43.688
It offers a thousand problems over seven libraries,

00:05:43.768 --> 00:05:47.919
drawn from four hundred fifty-one unique
StackOverflow posts.

00:05:47.999 --> 00:05:52.705
The best model reaches only forty-three point three
percent accuracy.

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:57.283
The evaluation is highly reliable: among all
solutions it accepts,

00:05:57.363 --> 00:06:00.890
just one point eight percent are actually incorrect,

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:05.398
and only about half a percent of rejected ones are
truly correct.

00:06:05.478 --> 00:06:09.144
And with a hundred forty words per problem on average,

00:06:09.224 --> 00:06:12.820
its contexts are far richer than comparable datasets.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.998
DS-1000 gives the community a data science code
benchmark that is realistic,

00:06:18.078 --> 00:06:21.207
reliably evaluated, and resistant to memorization.

00:06:21.288 --> 00:06:24.802
Because it is built from natural StackOverflow problems,

00:06:24.882 --> 00:06:29.488
judged by executing code against test cases and
surface-form constraints,

00:06:29.568 --> 00:06:34.943
and defended by deliberate perturbation, its scores
are trustworthy and hard to game.

00:06:35.023 --> 00:06:40.207
And because even the strongest model reaches only
forty-three point three percent,

00:06:40.287 --> 00:06:45.470
it makes clear how much room remains for progress
in data science code generation.
